<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berbera</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Berbera is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>. A trading port since antiquity, it was long famous for an annual fair, held in the cool season, where caravans from the interior met merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was the capital of British Somaliland until 1941, and its deep-water port remains Somaliland's main gateway.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berbera.jpg" alt="Berbera" width="320" height="169" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Seepsimon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berbera_6666.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Berbera lies on the coast of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, about 160 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hargeisa">Hargeisa</a>. It has the only sheltered harbour on the southern side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a>, and the surrounding coastal lowlands are semi-arid. The climate is hot and arid, with about 52 millimetres of rain a year. The name is traced by one account to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> phrase beri-beri, meaning occasionally, because the place was a seasonal settlement occupied in the cooler months; the Royal Asiatic Society suggested instead an Arabic word meaning talking much or shouting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Berbera is believed to be the port of Malao described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a>, a Greek merchant's guide of the first century CE, which lists exports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a>, frankincense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a harbour for the Adal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a> sultanates, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a> one of the two main ports of Adal. Ludovico di Varthema, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duarte-barbosa">Duarte Barbosa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a> described its trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khambhat">Cambay</a> in the early 16th century, and a Portuguese fleet sacked it and Zeila between 1516 and 1518.</p><p>In the 18th and 19th centuries it was the most important port of the Somali Peninsula. An annual fair between October and April drew Isaaq clans, caravans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> and the interior, and Indian merchants. In the 1833 season the town swelled to 70,000 people, and in 1848 duties on goods from Berbera supplied 80% of the revenue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>. Somali merchants brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> grown near Harar to Mokha and Aden as Berbera coffee. After a British ship was attacked in 1825 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> followed, the Habr Awal were defeated in a battle in 1827 and ordered to pay 15,000 Spanish dollars.</p><p>After treaties with the clans between 1884 and 1886 the British made Berbera the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of British Somaliland, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hargeisa">Hargeisa</a> replaced it in 1941. In August 1940 the British evacuated about 7,000 people through the port before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> occupied the territory, and they recaptured it in March 1941. After independence in 1960 Berbera was the Somali Republic's main livestock port, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> built port facilities and a military base there that were completed in 1969.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Berbera is the capital of the Sahil region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a> and its main sea port, linked by road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hargeisa">Hargeisa</a> and Burco. Its exports have included livestock, gum arabic, frankincense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a>, and goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> also pass through it. The port has been expanded under an agreement made in 2016 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dp-world">DP World</a>, and the city has an airport with a very long runway.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Berbera” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Somaliland is a dry country of pastoralists, and its economy rests on livestock, above all sheep, goats and camels, exported through the port of <b>Berbera</b> to Saudi Arabia and the Gulf states, especially for the hajj season.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1884 and 1886 British officers signed treaties with the Isaaq, Gadabuursi, Warsangali and other clans, and after Egypt withdrew from Zeila and <b>Berbera</b> the protectorate was established, run at first from British India.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-somaliland">British Somaliland</a></li><li><blockquote>Mohammed Abdullah Hassan, a scholar of the Salihiyya Sufi order, returned from Arabia in the mid-1890s and began preaching in <b>Berbera</b> against British rule and Christian missions.</blockquote><a href="/state/dervish-state">Dervish State</a></li><li><blockquote>Its people herded camels and traded through <b>Berbera</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/habr-yunis-sultanate">Habr Yunis Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The emirs minted coins and taxed the caravans carrying coffee, khat, slaves and ivory to Zeila and <b>Berbera</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/harar-emirate">Harar Emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Isaaq economy rested on camel and sheep herding and on trade through <b>Berbera</b>, whose annual fair drew merchants from Arabia and India.</blockquote><a href="/state/isaaq-sultanate">Isaaq Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
